    "seconds": 53,
    "summary": "The anomaly is mitigated: the `post-storage-service` Service was pointing at the wrong container port, and I corrected it.\n\n**What I found**\n- All 27 pods were Running with 0 restarts, so the problem was not pod health.\n- `compose-post-service` and `nginx-thrift` logged repeated `Connection refused` errors connecting to `post-storage-service:9090`. This broke the compose-post path.\n- The `post-storage-service` Service had `targetPort: 9999`, but the container listens on 9090 (`containerPort: 9090`, and the service config uses 9090). The Service resolved to an endpoint at `10.244.1.228:9999`, where nothing was listening.\n- Every other Service in the namespace maps port to targetPort 9090 (except the media-frontend port mapping 8081\u21928080, which I left alone).\n\n**What I changed**\n- Patched `svc/post-storage-service` to set `targetPort: 9090`. The endpoint is now `10.244.1.228:9090`.\n- In the 20 seconds after the patch, `compose-post-service` logged zero new \"Connection refused\" errors.\n\n**Caveats**\n- I did not send a live compose-post request, so the fix is confirmed by the absence of errors and the corrected endpoint, not an end-to-end test. There was no visible traffic during the check window.\n- The namespace was deployed with Helm (`meta.helm.sh/release-name: social-network`). My patch is a direct edit, so the next `helm upgrade` will likely restore `9999`. The fix should go into the chart values or templates as well.\n- `home-timeline-redis` logged a one-time `FailedMount` for its configmap at startup. The pod is Running and ready, so I did not change it.",
    "timed_out": false,
